\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{The scope of the finite gauge construction}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{The [positive convolution theorem](fdip-0080) and
[projected finite-time trace law](fdip-0083) concern a specified
pure-gauge model on a finite spatial graph. Their assumptions are
the strictly positive, summable character coefficients of
\ref{fdip-007Z}, not reflection positivity alone. The
[trace-square theorem](fdip-0086) applies to the stated algebra of
bounded gauge-invariant spatial observables.}

\p{Lüscher's construction includes #{SU(N)} gauge fields and
Wilson fermions. Proposition 1 proves self-adjointness, boundedness,
gauge invariance and strict positivity for that transfer operator;
its factorization and tensor-character argument motivate the
pure-gauge proof here. The fermion factors require the stated
hopping assumptions, including #{0<K_{\mathrm{hop}}<1/6};
#{K_{\mathrm{hop}}} is that paper's hopping parameter, not the
spatial gauge group #{K} used here.
See \citet{pp. 286–288, Proposition 1}{luscher1977transfer}.}

\p{Proposition 2 in
\citet{p. 289}{luscher1977transfer} identifies time-ordered
expectations under its specified gauge fixing and boundary
conditions: periodic gauge fields and anticyclic fermions in time.
The qualification on periodic temporal gauge appears in footnote 3
on p. 285. The direct projected integral of \ref{fdip-0083}
retains temporal averaging, rather than using that gauge-fixing
identification as a finite-periodic premise.}

\p{The [spectral Hamiltonian](fdip-0085) has its stated maximal
domain; boundedness of the transfer operator does not make its
logarithm bounded. No unique-vacuum or continuum reconstruction
claim is required. In particular, the [joint Gaussian matter
law](fdip-0076) and lattice fermion operators need their own
transfer and positivity hypotheses.}
